-- title:
--   The past, germ and tail sigma-fields of a process, and Lipschitz continuity at a point
-- statement:
--   The $\sigma$-field apparatus of chapter 7 of Durrett, *Probability: Theory and Examples*, for a
--   real-valued process $B$ indexed by $[0,\infty)$ on a measurable space $\Omega$, together with
--   the pointwise Lipschitz condition of Theorem 7.1.6.
--
--   **The past.** $\mathcal F^{0}_{t}=\sigma(B_s:s\le t)$, formed as the supremum, in the lattice of
--   $\sigma$-algebras on $\Omega$, of the pullbacks of the Borel $\sigma$-algebra along the maps
--   $B_s$ for $s\le t$.
--
--   **The germ.** $\mathcal F^{+}_{0}=\bigcap_{t>0}\mathcal F^{0}_{t}$, the infimum of the past
--   fields over all strictly positive times. An event lies in it exactly when it is decided by the
--   path on every initial interval $[0,t]$, however short.
--
--   **The future and the tail.** $\mathcal F'_{t}=\sigma(B_s:s\ge t)$, and
--   $\mathcal T=\bigcap_{t\ge0}\mathcal F'_{t}$.
--
--   **Lipschitz at a point.** A path $f:[0,\infty)\to\mathbb{R}$ is Lipschitz at $s$ with constant
--   $C$ when there is a scale $\delta>0$ such that
--   $$|f(t)-f(s)|\le C\,|t-s|\qquad\text{whenever }|t-s|\le\delta .$$
--
--   **Formalization Note** The index set is $[0,\infty)$, so at $s=0$ the Lipschitz condition is
--   one-sided, as it must be. A path differentiable at $s$ is Lipschitz at $s$ for some constant, so
--   the negation of this condition at every point and for every constant is a strictly stronger
--   statement than nowhere differentiability. For $C<0$ the condition is satisfiable only by a path
--   constant near $s$, which makes its negation trivially true there; the content of Theorem 7.1.6
--   is at $C\ge0$.
--
--   The bounded suprema and infima are over the *whole* index type, with the out-of-range terms
--   contributing the bottom and top $\sigma$-algebras respectively, which are the neutral elements;
--   the results are the ordinary generated and intersected $\sigma$-fields. No measurability of $B$
--   is assumed in these definitions: they are well-formed for any family of functions, and a
--   theorem that needs $\mathcal F^{0}_{t}$ to sit inside the ambient $\sigma$-field says so.
-- source:
--   Durrett, Probability: Theory and Examples, Version 5 (11 January 2019), chapter 7, pp. 358-364 (PDF pp. 366-372): the filtration of section 7.2, p. 360 (PDF p. 368) -- "F^o_s = sigma(B_r : r <= s) ... it is convenient to replace F^o_s by F^+_s = intersection over t > s of F^o_t" -- the tail sigma-field of p. 363 (PDF p. 371) -- "F'_t = sigma(B_s : s >= t) = the future at time t. T = intersection over t >= 0 of F'_t = the tail sigma-field" -- and the Lipschitz condition appearing in Theorem 7.1.6, p. 358 (PDF p. 366). sha256 aeac36cbf5e44c53d69fa60a2d29a393e2d0e8c955ee103bd845d925fd910886

import Mathlib

open Filter MeasureTheory ProbabilityTheory
open scoped NNReal Topology

namespace DurrettProbability

variable {Ω : Type*} [MeasurableSpace Ω]

/-- `pastSigma B t = σ(B_s : s ≤ t)`, Durrett's `F⁰_t`: the σ-field generated by the process up
to time `t`. Durrett, *Probability: Theory and Examples*, section 7.2. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def pastSigma (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ s ∈ Set.Iic t, MeasurableSpace.comap (B s) inferInstance

/-- `germSigma B = ⋂_{t > 0} σ(B_s : s ≤ t)`, Durrett's germ σ-field `F⁺₀`: the events decided by
the path on every initial interval, however short. Durrett, section 7.2, Theorem 7.2.3. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def germSigma (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨅ t ∈ Set.Ioi (0 : ℝ≥0), pastSigma B t

/-- `futureSigma B t = σ(B_s : s ≥ t)`, Durrett's `F'_t`, the future at time `t`. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def futureSigma (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) (t : ℝ≥0) : MeasurableSpace Ω :=
  ⨆ s ∈ Set.Ici t, MeasurableSpace.comap (B s) inferInstance

/-- `tailSigma B = ⋂_{t ≥ 0} σ(B_s : s ≥ t)`, Durrett's tail σ-field `T`.
Durrett, *Probability: Theory and Examples*, Theorem 7.2.7. -/
@[instance_reducible]
def tailSigma (B : ℝ≥0 → Ω → ℝ) : MeasurableSpace Ω := ⨅ t : ℝ≥0, futureSigma B t

/-- `LipschitzAtPoint f s C` says the path `f` is Lipschitz at the point `s` with constant `C`:
there is a scale `δ > 0` on which `|f t - f s| ≤ C |t - s|`. A path differentiable at `s` is
Lipschitz at `s`, so failure of this for every `s` and every `C` is failure of differentiability
everywhere. Durrett, *Probability: Theory and Examples*, Theorem 7.1.6. -/
def LipschitzAtPoint (f : ℝ≥0 → ℝ) (s : ℝ≥0) (C : ℝ) : Prop :=
  ∃ δ : ℝ, 0 < δ ∧ ∀ t : ℝ≥0, |(t : ℝ) - s| ≤ δ → |f t - f s| ≤ C * |(t : ℝ) - s|

end DurrettProbability


